⏺ I fixed it: slugifyWithCounter() now never hands out the same slug twice. npm test passes (xo and all 24 ava tests), and a random test of 2,000 runs with
 inputs like foo, foo 2 and foo 1 3 produced no duplicates.
 The cause: the counter only kept track of how many times it had seen each base slug. It didn't remember which slugs it had already returned. So once it gave
 out foo-2, a later foo 2 produced the plain slug foo-2, which looked new and was returned again.
 The fix (in index.js): the counter now also keeps a set of every slug it has returned. If the next slug is already in that set, it raises the number until it
 finds a free one. The set is cleared by reset(). Existing behaviour is unchanged: all the old counter tests pass as before.
 What you get now:
 - foo → foo, foo → foo-2, foo 2 → foo-2-2, foo 2 → foo-2-3, foo → foo-3
 - It works in the other order too: foo 2 → foo-2, foo → foo, foo → foo-3 (it skips the taken foo-2)
 I added both sequences to the counter test in test.js. Nothing is committed.
